#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180453} :activity/id #uuid "563e73f4-5c65-5c01-b1d4-1909214d3b23" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050891 :topic/id #uuid "6e3d5713-6e26-51db-8b8f-61d36b28e342" :topic/math-academy-id 3692} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180453 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180706 :tutorial/content "Recall that  if $f(x,y)$ is a function whose partial derivatives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y},\n$$\n\nexist and are continuous at a point $(x_0,y_0) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ then the gradient of $f$ evaluated at $(x_0, y_0)$ is given by\n\n \n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x_0,y_0) = \\dfrac{\\partial f }{\\partial x}(x_0, y_0) \\,\\mathbf{i} + \\dfrac{\\partial f }{\\partial y} (x_0, y_0)\\,\\mathbf{j} \\, .\n$$\n\n \n\nNow suppose that the gradient vector exists at every point $(x,y)$ contained within the set $U\\subset \\mathbb R^2.$ Then, the **gradient vector field** of $f$ is defined as the map\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\longmapsto \\nabla f(x,y), \\qquad (x,y)\\in U.\n$$\n\nAs an example, consider the function $f(x,y)=x^2-y^2.$ To find the gradient vector field of $f,$ we proceed as follows.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =2x\\,i-2y\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA sketch of this vector field is shown below.\n\n![](images/74/7437bd110810a0356a25afea897634c152b0441aee8e6403aa051dd2c1f205a7.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "fe62959b-3e46-5af2-ba87-08f376c38b4e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5764 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "42fa31ed-8f97-5aa3-89e8-9d271c417891" :step/math-academy-id 35370 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180458}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180454 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179718 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179580 :question/id #uuid "d83c960d-aeb2-5c31-b26f-f17518220ac0" :question/math-academy-id "e-9444"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "364d2c9a-6e9a-57f8-aa57-c10ee223061b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050033}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179977} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387310} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387317}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Relationship Between the Vector Field of a Function and Its Contour Plot"} :step/id #uuid "93326d12-a17a-5177-8940-426e5dd661b8" :step/math-academy-id 35372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180458 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179720 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179582 :question/id #uuid "2eaebefb-6b18-5689-a746-0d5dc595991c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9453"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd5c5fd8-409c-5b92-a0f1-4466c76cdbe0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050149}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256682} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387262}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient Vector Field of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "cc32145d-a804-5c13-ba20-8531d24745d0" :step/math-academy-id 35387 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180464}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180464 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179725 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179587 :question/id #uuid "d41ebc3c-f998-577e-a50b-a08faadd561f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9474"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a43f82e-6682-56af-994a-b0bdf12b4095" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048923}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256714} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383019} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387276}] :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Gradient Vector Field of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "f3f39866-fb9c-5fab-80a0-21104a970e7c" :step/math-academy-id 35460 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180466}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180466 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180711 :tutorial/content "Consider the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y) = x^2+y,$ shown below. How is this vector field related to the level curves of $f?$\n\n![](images/ef/ef5800723603b78beeb40c4352153f37298d4836905a6d8b751a919924664fa5.png)\n\nTo answer this question, we note the following:\n\n- As we've seen, the gradient of $f$ at a particular point $(x_0, y_0)$ lies *perpendicular* to the level curve $f(x,y) = f(x_0, y_0).$\n\n- Therefore, the gradient field vectors of $f$ at an arbitrary point $(x,y)$ lie perpendicular to its level curve at $(x,y).$\n\n- Moreover, the vectors always point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ is increasing.\n\nNow, if we superimpose the level curves of $f$ onto its vector field, we get a picture that looks as follows:\n\n![](images/5b/5bd2729b82312a50ee0c274fc1591545588f4881e2e279e80aca6b68f76ee824.png)\n\nNotice that the field vectors are indeed perpendicular to the level curves.\n\nWe can use a similar idea to sketch the vector field of a function using its level curves. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "60bb3f88-e435-5258-8b56-9c92481e7976" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5774 :tutorial/title "The Relationship Between the Vector Field of a Function and Its Contour Plot"} :step/id #uuid "7dce8a1b-d2dc-5ba7-9445-52bd966f8de5" :step/math-academy-id 35468 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180454}}] :activity/title "Gradient Vector Fields" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179852}]}
